  <ul style="list-style:none;line-height:2;margin:0;padding:0;">
    <li><strong>Capital:</strong> Urumqi (Ürümqi)</li>
    <li><strong>Population:</strong> 25 million (Uyghur, Han, Kazakh, Hui, and more)</li>
    <li><strong>Area:</strong> 1.66 million km² (larger than Alaska)</li>
    <li><strong>Best time to visit:</strong> May – October</li>
    <li><strong>Time zone:</strong> UTC+8 (officially), but locals use UTC+6 informally</li>
    <li><strong>Currency:</strong> Chinese Yuan (CNY/RMB)</li>
    <li><strong>Visa:</strong> Chinese tourist visa required for most nationalities</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Why Visit Xinjiang?</h2>

<p>Xinjiang is unlike anywhere else in China. Its Central Asian heritage, Turkic culture, and Islamic architecture feel more like Samarkand than Shanghai. Here's what makes it special:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Dramatic landscapes:</strong> From the turquoise lakes of Kanas to the flaming mountains of Turpan, from the vast Taklamakan Desert to the alpine meadows of Ili</li>
  <li><strong>Ancient Silk Road history:</strong> Kashgar's 2,000-year-old bazaar, the ruined city of Jiaohe, and the Buddhist grottoes of Bezeklik</li>
  <li><strong>Unique culture:</strong> Uyghur music and dance, traditional crafts, and a culinary tradition that blends Chinese, Central Asian, and Middle Eastern influences</li>
  <li><strong>Off-the-beaten-path:</strong> Despite its size, Xinjiang receives a fraction of the tourists of Beijing or Shanghai — you'll often have major sites to yourself</li>
  <li><strong>Adventure opportunities:</strong> Trekking in the Tianshan mountains, camel trekking in the desert, horse riding in Kazakh grasslands</li>
</ul>

<InlineCta />
<h2>Best Time to Visit Xinjiang</h2>

<p>Xinjiang's vast size means climate varies dramatically by region. Here's a month-by-month guide:</p>

<h3>Spring (April – May)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Flower viewing, fewer crowds, lower prices</p>
<p>April brings apricot and peach blossoms to the Ili Valley and Turpan. May sees the grasslands turn green and wildflowers bloom across the Tianshan. Temperatures are mild (15–25°C), though nights can still be cold in mountain areas. This is shoulder season, so hotels are cheaper and sites are less crowded.</p>

<h3>Summer (June – August)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Mountain areas, grasslands, longest days</p>
<p>Summer is peak season in Xinjiang. June and July are ideal for Kanas, Ili, and the Tianshan mountains — temperatures are pleasant (20–28°C) and the scenery is at its most lush. August can be hot in the desert areas (Turpan reaches 40°C+), but mountain areas remain comfortable. This is the most expensive and busiest time to visit.</p>

<h3>Autumn (September – October)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Golden foliage, harvest season, photography</p>
<p>Autumn is arguably the most beautiful season in Xinjiang. September brings golden poplar forests along the Tarim River and harvest season across the region. October sees the Kanas area transform into a canvas of red, orange, and gold. Temperatures are comfortable (10–20°C), and crowds thin out after the October National Day holiday. This is our favorite time to visit.</p>

<h3>Winter (November – March)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Winter sports, cultural experiences, budget travel</p>
<p>Winter is off-season in Xinjiang. The Kanas area becomes a winter wonderland with snow-covered forests and frozen lakes. Urumqi and the Tianshan offer excellent skiing. However, many remote areas are inaccessible due to snow, and some hotels close for the season. Prices are at their lowest, and you'll have most sites to yourself.</p>

<h2>Top Destinations in Xinjiang</h2>

<h3>1. Kashgar (喀什)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Culture, history, bazaars, Silk Road atmosphere</p>
<p>Kashgar is the heart of Uyghur culture and the most Silk Road-feeling city in China. Its 2,000-year-old Old City is a maze of mud-brick houses, mosques, and artisan workshops. Don't miss the Id Kah Mosque (China's largest), the Sunday Animal Market (one of Central Asia's last great nomad markets), and the Abakh Hoja Tomb (known as the "Fragrant Concubine's Tomb"). Plan at least 2–3 days here to soak in the atmosphere.</p>

<h3>2. Urumqi (乌鲁木齐)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Gateway city, museums, day trips</p>
<p>As Xinjiang's capital and main transport hub, most travelers start or end their journey in Urumqi. While not a major tourist destination itself, the Xinjiang Regional Museum is excellent — its mummy collection (including the 3,800-year-old "Loulan Beauty") is world-class. Urumqi is also a good base for day trips to Heavenly Lake (Tianchi) and the Southern Pastures.</p>

<h3>3. Turpan (吐鲁番)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Ancient ruins, desert landscapes, fruit, extreme heat</p>
<p>Turpan is one of the hottest and lowest places on Earth (154m below sea level), with summer temperatures regularly exceeding 40°C. But its historical sites are unmatched: the ruined city of Jiaohe (a 2,300-year-old Silk Road oasis carved into a river plateau), the Bezeklik Thousand Buddha Caves (with 5th–14th century Buddhist murals), and the Flaming Mountains (made famous by the Chinese classic "Journey to the West"). Turpan is also famous for its grapes — visit the Grape Valley and try the region's legendary raisins.</p>

<h3>4. Kanas (喀纳斯)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Nature, trekking, autumn foliage, photography</p>
<p>Located in Xinjiang's far north near the Russian and Mongolian borders, Kanas is one of China's most beautiful natural areas. The star is Kanas Lake — a turquoise, glacier-fed lake surrounded by pristine forests of Siberian larch and birch. The area is home to the Tuvan people, a Turkic ethnic group with roots in Mongolia. Visit in late September/early October for the spectacular golden autumn foliage. Plan at least 3–4 days, as getting here requires a long drive or flight from Urumqi.</p>

<h3>5. Ili Valley (伊犁河谷)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Grasslands, flowers, horse riding, Kazakh culture</p>
<p>The Ili Valley in western Xinjiang is a lush, green paradise surrounded by snow-capped mountains. It's home to some of China's most beautiful grasslands — Nalati, Kalajun, and Zhaosu. In June and July, the grasslands are carpeted with wildflowers and filled with Kazakh nomads grazing their herds. Stay in a traditional yurt, go horse riding, and experience authentic nomadic culture. The area is also famous for its lavender fields (in Huocheng County) which bloom in mid-June.</p>

<h3>6. Taklamakan Desert (塔克拉玛干沙漠)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Desert landscapes, camel trekking, star gazing</p>
<p>The Taklamakan is China's largest desert and the world's second-largest shifting sand desert. Its name means "go in and you won't come out" in Uyghur — a warning about its vast, unforgiving dunes. But the desert offers incredible experiences: camel trekking, overnight desert camping under some of the clearest skies in China, and visits to ancient oasis towns like Hotan (famous for jade and carpets). The best way to experience the Taklamakan is to drive the Desert Highway — a 522km road that cuts straight through the dunes.</p>

<h3>7. Hotan (和田)</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Jade, carpets, traditional crafts, authentic Uyghur culture</p>
<p>Hotan (Hetian) is an ancient oasis city on the southern edge of the Taklamakan Desert, famous for 4,000 years of jade production. The Hotan Jade Market is a fascinating place to watch traders negotiate over raw jade stones. The city is also known for its hand-knotted silk carpets, traditional paper making, and the lively Sunday bazaar. Hotan feels less touristy than Kashgar and offers a more authentic glimpse of Uyghur life.</p>

<h2>Sample Xinjiang Itineraries</h2>

<h3>7-Day Northern Xinjiang: Urumqi – Turpan – Urumqi</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> First-time visitors, limited time, cultural and historical highlights</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Arrive Urumqi, visit Xinjiang Regional Museum, explore International Grand Bazaar</li>
  <li><strong>Day 2:</strong> Day trip to Heavenly Lake (Tianchi), afternoon return to Urumqi</li>
  <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> High-speed train to Turpan, visit Jiaohe Ruins and Flaming Mountains</li>
  <li><strong>Day 4:</strong> Bezeklik Caves, Grape Valley, Emin Minaret, night train back to Urumqi</li>
  <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Arrive Urumqi, day trip to Southern Pastures, Kazakh yurt experience</li>
  <li><strong>Day 6:</strong> Free day in Urumqi, shopping for souvenirs, food tour</li>
  <li><strong>Day 7:</strong> Departure</li>
</ul>

<h3>10-Day Classic Xinjiang: Urumqi – Turpan – Kashgar</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Deep cultural experience, Silk Road history, desert and oasis</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Days 1–2:</strong> Urumqi (museum, Heavenly Lake, bazaar)</li>
  <li><strong>Days 3–4:</strong> Turpan (Jiaohe, Bezeklik, Flaming Mountains, Grape Valley)</li>
  <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Flight or overnight train to Kashgar</li>
  <li><strong>Days 6–8:</strong> Kashgar (Old City, Id Kah Mosque, Sunday Market, day trip to Karakul Lake)</li>
  <li><strong>Day 9:</strong> Flight back to Urumqi</li>
  <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> Departure</li>
</ul>

<h3>14-Day Northern Xinjiang Nature: Urumqi – Kanas – Ili</h3>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Nature lovers, photographers, trekking, autumn foliage</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Days 1–2:</strong> Urumqi arrival and exploration</li>
  <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Flight to Burqin, drive to Kanas</li>
  <li><strong>Days 4–6:</strong> Kanas Lake, Hemu Village, trekking, photography</li>
  <li><strong>Day 7:</strong> Drive to Burqin, flight to Yining</li>
  <li><strong>Days 8–10:</strong> Ili Valley (Nalati Grassland, Kalajun, yurt stay, horse riding)</li>
  <li><strong>Day 11:</strong> Drive to Urumqi (or flight)</li>
  <li><strong>Days 12–13:</strong> Urumqi and Turpan highlights</li>
  <li><strong>Day 14:</strong> Departure</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting Around Xinjiang</h2>

<h3>Flights</h3>
<p>Xinjiang has several airports with regular domestic connections. Urumqi is the main hub, with direct flights to Kashgar, Hotan, Yining, Burqin (Kanas), and other regional airports. Flying is the most efficient way to cover Xinjiang's vast distances — Urumqi to Kashgar is 2 hours by air vs. 20+ hours by train or car.</p>

<h3>Trains</h3>
<p>Xinjiang's rail network is expanding rapidly. The Lanzhou–Urumqi high-speed rail line connects Urumqi to Turpan (1 hour), Hami (3 hours), and Lanzhou (12 hours). The conventional Southern Xinjiang Railway connects Urumqi to Kashgar (20–24 hours) with overnight sleeper trains. The Golmud–Korla railway connects Xinjiang to Qinghai and Tibet. Overnight trains are a great way to save on accommodation and experience Chinese train travel.</p>

<h3>Private Car with Driver</h3>
<p>For most travelers, a private car with driver is the best way to explore Xinjiang. Distances are vast, public transport is limited in remote areas, and having a local driver who knows the roads is invaluable. Expect to pay $120–200/day for a comfortable SUV with driver (includes fuel, tolls, and driver's accommodation). For groups of 4–6, this works out to be very cost-effective.</p>

<h3>Public Transport</h3>
<p>Long-distance buses connect major cities, but they're slow and uncomfortable for multi-day travel. Within cities, taxis and ride-hailing apps (Didi) are affordable and convenient. In remote areas, public transport is essentially non-existent — you'll need a private car or organized tour.</p>

<h2>Where to Stay in Xinjiang</h2>

<h3>Urumqi</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Luxury:</strong> Sheraton Urumqi Hotel, Wanda Vista Urumqi ($150–300/night)</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-range:</strong> Hoi Tak Hotel, Urumqi Marriott ($80–150/night)</li>
  <li><strong>Budget:</strong> International Youth Hostel, local guesthouses ($25–50/night)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Kashgar</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Luxury:</strong> Kashgar Grand Hotel, Radisson Blu Kashgar ($120–250/night)</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-range:</strong> Kashgar Old City Youth Hostel, boutique hotels in the Old City ($50–100/night)</li>
  <li><strong>Budget:</strong> Hostels and guesthouses near the bus station ($20–40/night)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Kanas & Ili</h3>
<p>In remote areas, accommodation options are more limited. Kanas has guesthouses and wooden cabins inside the scenic area ($80–200/night), plus more options in Burqin town. Ili grasslands offer traditional yurt stays ($30–80/night, including meals) — a unique and memorable experience. Book well in advance for peak season (June–September), as options sell out quickly.</p>

<h2>What to Eat in Xinjiang</h2>

<p>Xinjiang cuisine is one of China's most distinctive and delicious culinary traditions. It blends Central Asian, Middle Eastern, and Chinese influences, with lamb, naan bread, and hand-pulled noodles as staples.</p>

<h3>Must-Try Dishes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lamb kebabs (烤羊肉串):</strong> The ultimate Xinjiang street food — chunks of lamb grilled over charcoal with cumin and chili</li>
  <li><strong>Big plate chicken (大盘鸡):</strong> A whole chicken braised with potatoes, peppers, and noodles — served on a platter the size of a steering wheel</li>
  <li><strong>Pilaf (抓饭):</strong> Fragrant rice cooked with lamb, carrots, and cumin — traditionally eaten with the right hand</li>
  <li><strong>Laghman (拉条子):</strong> Hand-pulled noodles served with a spicy lamb and vegetable stir-fry</li>
  <li><strong>Naan (馕):</strong> Round flatbread baked in tandoor ovens — the daily bread of Xinjiang</li>
  <li><strong>Samsa (烤包子):</strong> Baked pastries stuffed with minced lamb and onions</li>
  <li><strong>Yogurt (酸奶):</strong> Thick, creamy, and tangy — often served with honey or sugar</li>
  <li><strong>Fruits:</strong> Xinjiang is famous for its sweet melons, grapes, apricots, and figs — thanks to the hot days and cool nights</li>
</ul>

